---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import P from "../components/P.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import qld from "../data/qld-bdm.json";
const deaths = qld.filter((r) => r.type === "death");
const marr = qld.filter((r) => r.type === "marriage");
---
<Base title="The Queensland Register" eyebrow="Read 10 September 2026"
      dek="The Registry's historical index is free, it is authoritative, and — the part that matters — it can be searched by a father's or a mother's name. One search returned three of Sebastiano Mazza's siblings.">

  <section class="blk">
    <p class="lede">The Queensland Registry of Births, Deaths and Marriages publishes a searchable
    historical index: <strong>deaths to 1996, marriages to 1951, births to 1926</strong>. Every death
    and birth entry carries <strong>the names of both parents</strong>, and the search box will take a
    parent's name as the query.</p>
    <p class="lede" style="margin-top:1rem">That turns it from a lookup into a <em>reconstruction
    tool</em>. Searching <strong>"Salvatore Mazza"</strong> in the father field returns the deaths of
    his children — and each of those names their mother, which is how a family asserted by the tree
    became a family on the record.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">The finding</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Michele Mazza was Sebastiano's brother</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p><P n="Michele Mazza" bold={true} /> landed at Brisbane on the <em>Palermo</em> on
      <strong>21 April 1925</strong>, aged about sixteen, and died the following year. The family tree
      asserted he was kin and produced nothing to show it. The archive has carried the question since
      the day it was built: <em>was he the reason they chose Queensland?</em></p>
      <p>The Registry's index answers it:</p>
      <blockquote style="margin:1rem 0"><strong>Michali Mazza</strong> · death registration
      <strong>14 November 1926</strong> · <strong>1926/C/3983</strong> ·
      father <strong>Salvatori Mazza</strong> · mother <strong>Maria Lecota</strong>.</blockquote>
      <p><em>Lecota</em> is the index's rendering of <strong>Nicotra</strong> — and the same search
      returns <strong>Rosario Mazza, born 1899</strong>, whose parents are given in full as
      <strong>Salvatore Mazza and Maria Concetta Nicotra</strong>. The same couple, spelled properly.</p>
      <p>So <strong>Michele Mazza was a son of Salvatore Mazza and Maria Nicotra</strong>, and
      therefore <P n="Sebastiano Mazza" />'s <strong>elder brother</strong>. He came out first, alone,
      at sixteen. He was dead within nineteen months. <strong>Twenty-four years later his family
      followed him to the port he had landed at.</strong></p>
      <p class="note" style="margin-top:1.2rem">The archive should say what it still cannot: whether
      anyone was waiting for Sebastiano in 1949, and whether the family knew where the boy was buried.
      The index gives a date and a registration number, not a grave. <strong>C</strong> in the
      registration means a country event — he did not die in Brisbane.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>Four deaths, and what each one proves</h2>
    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:16px">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th class="m">Date</th><th>Name</th><th class="m">Registration</th><th>Father</th><th>Mother</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>{deaths.map((r) => (
          <tr>
            <td class="m">{r.date}</td>
            <td><strong>{r.name}</strong></td>
            <td class="m">{r.reg}</td>
            <td>{r.father}</td>
            <td>{r.mother}</td>
          </tr>))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div class="stack" style="margin-top:22px">
      {deaths.map((r) => (
        <p class="qn" style="max-width:88ch"><b>{r.name}</b> — {r.note}</p>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>And two marriages</h2>
    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:16px">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th class="m">Date</th><th>Name</th><th>Married</th><th class="m">Registration</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>{marr.map((r) => (
          <tr><td class="m">{r.date}</td><td><strong>{r.name}</strong></td>
              <td>{r.spouse}</td><td class="m">{r.reg}</td></tr>))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div class="stack" style="margin-top:22px">
      {marr.map((r) => <p class="qn" style="max-width:88ch"><b>{r.name}</b> — {r.note}</p>)}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Where it points next</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Rosario, born 1899, dates his father</h2>
    <p class="dek" style="margin-top:.9rem">This archive still cannot find
    <strong>Salvatore Mazza's birth</strong> — six years of the Piedimonte Etneo register, plus
    Mascali and Giarre, and he is in none of them.</p>
    <p class="dek" style="margin-top:1rem">The Queensland index has just handed over the way in.
    <strong>Rosario Mazza was born in 1899</strong> to Salvatore and Maria Concetta Nicotra. An
    Italian birth act states the declaring father's <strong>age</strong>. Find Rosario's act of birth
    — Piedimonte Etneo, 1899 — and it dates Salvatore to within a year or two, which turns a blind
    sweep of the register into a single lookup. <a href={u("/open-questions/")}>The queue →</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk" style="border-bottom:0">
    <h2>Method</h2>
    <p class="dek" style="margin-top:.8rem">
    <code>familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au</code> — free to search, no account needed; images are paid and
    were not bought. The form's useful fields are <strong>Father's/parent's name(s)</strong> and
    <strong>Mother's/parent's name(s)</strong>, which work on births and deaths only. Search the
    <em>parent</em>, not the person, when you are trying to find siblings. Spellings in the index are
    as the clerk wrote them — <em>Michali</em>, <em>Salvatori</em>, <em>Lecota</em>,
    <em>Postamo</em> — so search loosely and expect the surname you want to be mangled.</p>
  </section>
</Base>
